# CapyResume configuration.
#
# There is nothing secret to put here. CapyResume has no database and no auth
# provider, and no API key is ever configured on a server: the resume lives in
# the user's browser, and the PDF, DOCX and JSON are produced locally. BYOK keys
# are entered by the user and sent straight from their browser to the provider
# they choose, so they never belong in this file.
#
# Do not add secrets here. If a paid tier ever lands, its server-side config
# belongs in a separate, gitignored file - never in this example.

# The origin the site is served from, with no trailing slash.
#
# Leave this unset for local development. The app runs fine without it, and that
# is the default `npm run dev` experience.
#
# Set it once the site is deployed, to its real origin. It is read at build time
# and only affects absolute URLs, so setting it needs no code change. While it is
# unset there is no origin to point at, and the app degrades rather than inventing
# one: the sitemap is emitted empty, `robots.txt` omits its `Sitemap:` line,
# canonicals fall back to relative hrefs, and social image URLs point at
# localhost. README.md's Environment table is the authoritative per-surface list;
# this comment only carries the rule and the warning below.
#
# A wrong origin is worse than a missing one here: it would emit canonicals and
# a sitemap pointing somewhere the site is not, so do not guess or commit a
# placeholder. `npm run verify:deploy <origin>` checks a build against the real
# one before it ships.
#
# Locally, copy this file to `.env.local` (gitignored) and uncomment the line.
# In production, set it in the hosting provider's environment settings instead -
# no file is needed, and nothing here has to be committed.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://your-domain.example
